{"subjectId":"python","id":"stdlib-tour","title":"Standard Library Tour","group":"Standard Library","order":55,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">“Batteries included”: before installing a package, check the standard library. A map of the modules worth knowing, with a tiny example of each.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-map\"></i>Where things live</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Module(s)</th><th>Covered in</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Containers &amp; algorithms</td><td><code>collections</code>, <code>heapq</code>, <code>bisect</code>, <code>array</code></td><td><em>Data Structures</em></td></tr><tr><td>Iteration &amp; functions</td><td><code>itertools</code>, <code>functools</code>, <code>operator</code></td><td><em>Advanced Python</em>, <em>Functions</em></td></tr><tr><td>Text</td><td><code>string</code>, <code>re</code>, <code>textwrap</code>, <code>difflib</code>, <code>unicodedata</code></td><td><em>Strings &amp; Numbers</em></td></tr><tr><td>Numbers</td><td><code>math</code>, <code>decimal</code>, <code>fractions</code>, <code>statistics</code>, <code>random</code></td><td><em>Numbers</em>, below</td></tr><tr><td>Files &amp; formats</td><td><code>pathlib</code>, <code>shutil</code>, <code>json</code>, <code>csv</code>, <code>tomllib</code>, <code>pickle</code>, <code>sqlite3</code>, <code>zipfile</code></td><td><em>Errors, Modules &amp; Files</em></td></tr><tr><td>Dates</td><td><code>datetime</code>, <code>zoneinfo</code>, <code>time</code>, <code>calendar</code></td><td><em>Dates &amp; Times</em></td></tr><tr><td>OS &amp; processes</td><td><code>os</code>, <code>sys</code>, <code>subprocess</code>, <code>argparse</code>, <code>logging</code></td><td>this group</td></tr><tr><td>Concurrency</td><td><code>threading</code>, <code>multiprocessing</code>, <code>concurrent.futures</code>, <code>asyncio</code>, <code>queue</code></td><td><em>Concurrency</em></td></tr><tr><td>Testing &amp; debugging</td><td><code>unittest</code>, <code>doctest</code>, <code>pdb</code>, <code>timeit</code>, <code>cProfile</code>, <code>tracemalloc</code></td><td><em>Testing &amp; Tooling</em></td></tr><tr><td>Security &amp; ids</td><td><code>hashlib</code>, <code>hmac</code>, <code>secrets</code>, <code>uuid</code>, <code>base64</code></td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>Internet (no network needed to parse)</td><td><code>urllib.parse</code>, <code>http</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>html</code>, <code>ipaddress</code></td><td>below</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-dice\"></i>random &amp; statistics</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import random, statistics\n\nrandom.seed(42)                         # same seed → same sequence (reproducible experiments)\nfirst = [random.randint(1, 6) for _ in range(5)]\nrandom.seed(42)\nprint(first == [random.randint(1, 6) for _ in range(5)])   # True\n\nrng = random.Random(7)                  # an independent generator with its own state\ndeck = list(\"AKQJ\")\nrng.shuffle(deck)                       # in place\nprint(len(rng.sample(range(100), 3)), rng.choice(deck) in deck)   # 3 True\n\ndata = [2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9]\nprint(statistics.mean(data), statistics.median(data), statistics.mode(data))   # 5 4.5 4\nprint(statistics.pstdev(data), round(statistics.stdev(data), 3))             # 2.0 2.138</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>random</code> is predictable by design — never use it for passwords, tokens or anything security-related. Use <code>secrets</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock\"></i>secrets, hashlib, uuid, base64</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import secrets, hashlib, hmac, uuid, base64\n\ntoken = secrets.token_urlsafe(16)                  # unpredictable, URL-safe\nprint(len(token) &gt;= 16)                            # True\nprint(secrets.compare_digest(\"abc\", \"abc\"))        # True   ← constant-time comparison\n\nprint(hashlib.sha256(b\"hello\").hexdigest()[:16])   # 2cf24dba5fb0a30e\nsig = hmac.new(b\"secret-key\", b\"payload\", hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()\nprint(len(sig))                                    # 64\n\nu = uuid.uuid4()                                   # random unique id\nprint(u.version, len(str(u)))                      # 4 36\n\nencoded = base64.b64encode(b\"hi there\")\nprint(encoded, base64.b64decode(encoded))          # b'aGkgdGhlcmU=' b'hi there'</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Hash passwords with a slow, salted algorithm — <code>hashlib.scrypt</code> or <code>pbkdf2_hmac</code> (or the <code>argon2</code>/<code>bcrypt</code> packages) — never plain <code>sha256</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-text-wrap\"></i>Text helpers: string, textwrap, difflib, pprint</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import string, textwrap, difflib, pprint\n\nprint(string.ascii_lowercase[:5], string.digits, string.punctuation[:4])   # abcde 0123456789 !\"#$\nprint(textwrap.shorten(\"The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog\", width=25))   # The quick brown fox [...]\nprint(textwrap.wrap(\"one two three four five\", width=9))   # ['one two', 'three', 'four five']\nprint(difflib.get_close_matches(\"pyhton\", [\"python\", \"java\", \"ruby\"]))   # ['python']\nprint(round(difflib.SequenceMatcher(None, \"kitten\", \"sitting\").ratio(), 2))   # 0.62\nprint(pprint.pformat({\"b\": [1, 2], \"a\": {\"x\": 1}}))        # {'a': {'x': 1}, 'b': [1, 2]}   ← keys sorted</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-world-www\"></i>urllib.parse, ipaddress, html</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from urllib.parse import urlparse, parse_qs, urlencode, quote\nimport ipaddress, html\n\nu = urlparse(\"https://shop.example.com:8443/search?q=red+shoes&amp;page=2#top\")\nprint(u.scheme, u.hostname, u.port, u.path)        # https shop.example.com 8443 /search\nprint(parse_qs(u.query))                           # {'q': ['red shoes'], 'page': ['2']}\nprint(urlencode({\"q\": \"café &amp; co\", \"n\": 3}))       # q=caf%C3%A9+%26+co&amp;n=3\nprint(quote(\"a b/c\"))                              # a%20b/c\n\nnet = ipaddress.ip_network(\"10.0.0.0/30\")\nprint([str(h) for h in net.hosts()])               # ['10.0.0.1', '10.0.0.2']\nprint(ipaddress.ip_address(\"192.168.1.5\").is_private)   # True\nprint(html.escape(\"&lt;b&gt;Tom &amp; Jerry&lt;/b&gt;\"))           # &amp;lt;b&amp;gt;Tom &amp;amp; Jerry&amp;lt;/b&amp;gt;</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-file-zip\"></i>Compression &amp; archives in memory</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import gzip, io, zipfile\n\nraw = b\"python \" * 1000\npacked = gzip.compress(raw)\nprint(len(raw), len(packed) &lt; 100, gzip.decompress(packed) == raw)   # 7000 True True\n\nbuf = io.BytesIO()\nwith zipfile.ZipFile(buf, \"w\", compression=zipfile.ZIP_DEFLATED) as zf:\n    zf.writestr(\"notes/a.txt\", \"alpha\")\n    zf.writestr(\"notes/b.txt\", \"beta\")\nwith zipfile.ZipFile(buf) as zf:\n    print(zf.namelist(), zf.read(\"notes/b.txt\"))   # ['notes/a.txt', 'notes/b.txt'] b'beta'</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tools\"></i>weakref, contextvars, dis</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import weakref, dis\n\nclass Big:\n    pass\n\nobj = Big()\nref = weakref.ref(obj)             # doesn't keep obj alive\nprint(ref() is obj)                # True\ndel obj\nprint(ref())                       # None   ← freed as soon as the last strong reference went\n\ncache = weakref.WeakValueDictionary()   # entries vanish when values are garbage-collected\n\ndef add(a, b):\n    return a + b\nops = [i.opname for i in dis.get_instructions(add)]\nprint(\"BINARY_OP\" in ops)          # True   ← dis shows the bytecode CPython runs</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-variable\"></i><code>contextvars</code></h3><p>Per-task “global” state (request id, user) that works correctly with asyncio, unlike thread-locals.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy\"></i><code>copy</code></h3><p><code>copy.copy</code> / <code>deepcopy</code> / <code>replace</code> (3.13).</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i><code>enum</code>, <code>dataclasses</code>, <code>typing</code></h3><p>Model data clearly — see <em>Object-Oriented Python</em>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i><code>sqlite3</code></h3><p>A full SQL database in a file (or in memory), no server needed.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-server\"></i><code>http.server</code></h3><p><code>python -m http.server 8000</code> serves the current folder — handy for quick tests.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box-multiple\"></i><code>venv</code>, <code>pip</code>, <code>ensurepip</code></h3><p>Environments and installers ship with Python.</p></div></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption>Handy `python -m` one-liners</figcaption><pre><code>$ python -m http.server 8000        # static file server\n$ python -m json.tool data.json     # pretty-print / validate JSON\n$ python -m timeit \"sum(range(1000))\"\n$ python -m venv .venv              # create a virtual environment\n$ python -m zipfile -l bundle.zip   # list an archive\n$ python -m calendar 2026 9         # print a month calendar</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When should you reach for a third-party package instead of the standard library?</summary><div><p>When the stdlib version is low-level or missing features you need: <code>requests</code>/<code>httpx</code> over <code>urllib.request</code>, <code>pandas</code> over <code>csv</code> for analysis, <code>pydantic</code> for validation, <code>rich</code>/<code>click</code> for CLIs, <code>dateutil</code> for fuzzy date parsing. Start with the stdlib — it has no install step and is stable.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>random vs secrets?</summary><div><p><code>random</code> uses the Mersenne Twister — fast and reproducible with a seed, great for simulations and tests, but predictable. <code>secrets</code> uses the OS's cryptographically secure source — use it for tokens, passwords and keys.</p></div></details></section>"}